#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# Cross-demo copy check (K-623, K-607, K-624).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The eight demos are eight standalone Rails apps, so shared
# application code is not shared at all — it is HAND-COPIED. `lib/demo_telemetry.rb`
# ships in seven demos, `app/controllers/demo_activity_controller.rb` in seven,
# `lib/pow_difficulty.rb` in seven, `lib/uuid_check.rb` in six, and eleven
# db/migrate files in up to seven. Nothing checked that the copies still agree.
#
# They drift, and the drift is invisible. `lib/demo_telemetry.rb` carried a stale
# `schedule_delivery` comment in six of its seven copies for weeks — corrected
# only by accident, when the K-622 fix happened to touch all seven at once. That
# left the seven byte-identical again, which is a STATE, not a GUARD: the next
# edit that lands in one copy and not the others is exactly as undetectable, and
# the next one may be executable rather than a comment. `lib/equihash_register.rb`
# is the proof that it need not be a comment — a constant rename reached three of
# its five copies (dbeeb97) and stopped there, unnoticed, until this check was
# written.
#
# So this is a CHECK, not a convention: it derives every relative path that
# exists in two or more demos, and fails when a copy disagrees with its siblings
# in a way nobody declared. "Someone must remember to propagate the edit" becomes
# "CI notices when they did not". Same shape, and the same reason, as
# bin/check-ci-tasks (K-619) — this is a SIBLING rather than a new mode of that
# script because the two answer different questions from different inputs: that
# one compares ci.yml against each demo's rake tasks, this one compares demo
# files against each other, and folding them together would put two unrelated
# invariants behind one exit code and one --write contract.
#
# THE RULE IT ENFORCES. Every relative path found in >= 2 demos must appear in
# the COPIES manifest below with a rule and a reason:
#
#   :identical — byte-for-byte in every demo that ships it.
#   :code      — identical once comments and whitespace are normalised away, so
#                each copy's header prose may name its own demo. Ripper does the
#                stripping, so a `#` inside a string or regex is not a comment.
#                MAGIC comments are the exception and are compared (K-628).
#   :per_demo  — a genuine per-demo implementation that shares only a filename.
#                Not compared; the manifest entry records WHY they differ, so the
#                next reader does not have to diff seven files to find out.
#
# `except:` is the escape hatch for one copy that legitimately differs from its
# siblings (getgrocery's uuid_check declares an extra JSON-Schema constant;
# stylish's stub_user_idp is a DB-backed staff IdP). It is a demo => reason map,
# the same shape as `ungated:` in ci.yml's demos matrix, and it is checked too:
# an exception for a demo that no longer ships the file, or whose copy has since
# converged on its siblings, is an error rather than a comfortable lie.
#
# A path that turns up in a second demo with no manifest entry fails the build.
# That is the property that matters: a NEW copy cannot appear silently, which is
# how six of these got here.
#
# WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not an argument that copying is right. Promoting
# UuidCheck or DemoTelemetry into a shipped gem is a public-API decision (K-607,
# K-495/K-502) and this check does not pre-empt it — it only makes the interim
# state honest, and it is what should be deleted the day the extraction lands.
#
# MAGIC COMMENTS ARE THE ONE COMMENT `:code` STILL COMPARES (K-628). Ripper drops
# every comment, magic ones included, so until this was added a copy that lost
# `# frozen_string_literal: true` compared equal to one that had it — and three of
# the four application_controller.rb copies had lost it, unnoticed, because a
# magic comment is the one comment that changes how the file RUNS. So the leading
# magic-comment block is extracted and compared separately, on the recognised
# Ruby set only (see MAGIC_COMMENTS): prose stays ignored, `# frozen_string_literal`
# does not. `:identical` needs nothing here — byte equality already covers it.
#
# Stdlib only (digest + ripper), no bundle, no database, ~0.2 s on a bare
# checkout — the same profile as bin/check-ci-tasks.
#
# Usage:
#   bin/check-demo-copies          # check
#   bin/check-demo-copies --list   # print the manifest as a table

require "digest"
require "ripper"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)

# Where hand-written demo code lives. Deliberately NOT the Rails skeleton
# (bin/, config/, public/, Rakefile, config.ru): those are generator output that
# each demo edits for its own port, host and initializer, and comparing them
# would drown the signal this check exists for. `lib/tasks/*.rake` is excluded
# too — per-demo by construction, and already covered by bin/check-ci-tasks.
SCAN_ROOTS = ["lib", "app", "script", "db/migrate"].freeze

# ── The manifest ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
